{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a91be9b723a1b0d7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E004 (RHAISTRAT-9014-E004) bundles code implementation with substantive\n10\t    content authoring: the scope item Author the integration-side documentation: how\n11\t    to enable and configure TrustyAI guardrails on a GA distribution, supported options,\n12\t    and behavior is a new doc topic, not an incidental touch-up falling out of the\n13\t    code change. Per the work-product type boundary rule, code implementation and\n14\t    documentation authoring must be separate epics even when owned by the same team.\n15\t    Correction: remove the doc-authoring scope item and its Integration documentation\n16\t    exists covering enablement, configuration options... AC from E004, leaving at\n17\t    most an AC that the promoted configuration surface is handed to documentation.'\n18\t- severity: major\n19\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n20\t  description: 'Scope duplication between E004 and E007: both epics author the TrustyAI\n21\t    guardrails configuration documentation (E004 AC Integration documentation exists\n22\t    covering enablement, configuration options, and behavior when the guardrails service\n23\t    is unavailable vs E007 scope The TrustyAI guardrails integration as a documented,\n24\t    supported configuration path and content outline item 5, published to GA standards\n25\t    under E007 first AC). Correction: consolidate the guardrails doc topic in E007,\n26\t    the docs-authoring epic, and add HLR-4 to E007 HLR Traceability so the documented-integration\n27\t    half of HLR-4 stays covered after it is removed from E004.'\n28\terror: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9014 (Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability)\n31\t\n32\t## Review Summary\n33\t\n34\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n35\tRecommendation: revise\n36\t\n37\tContract validator: OK (exit 0, no VIOLATION lines). Triage consistency: `triage: null`\n38\twith 7 epics — full decomposition, multi-epic output expected, no Step 0 contradiction.\n39\tDiagram/frontmatter cross-check: all 8 DAG edges match the epic `dependencies` lists\n40\texactly, in the dependency-to-dependent direction. One major Epic Boundaries issue\n41\t(E004 mixes code implementation with substantive content authoring, duplicating\n42\tE007's guardrails documentation scope); that criterion scores 0, which triggers the\n43\tauto-fail rule regardless of the total score.\n44\t\n45\t## Criterion Details\n46\t\n47\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n48\t\n49\tAll six HLRs map to at least one epic: HLR-1 → E001, HLR-2 → E002, HLR-3 → E003,\n50\tHLR-4 → E004 + E005, HLR-5 → E006, HLR-6 → E007. The P2 HLR-6 is delivered (not\n51\tdeferred) by the docs-authoring epic. Traceability matrix present and complete.\n52\t\n53\tPriority handling is correct throughout: E001 is P0 because it gates all P0\n54\tstability work (inheritance). E007 is a `docs-authoring` epic whose priority is\n55\tcorrectly derived from the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0) under\n56\tthe docs-authoring exception, so the P1 → P0 edges from E004/E005 into E007 are\n57\tnot priority inheritance violations. No priority collapse: every epic maps to\n58\tHLRs at a single priority level. E002's absorption of the assessment's\n59\tgap-closure scope is same-priority (P0) follow-through of HLR-1's inventory and\n60\tis recorded in the matrix row, so no `bundled` flag is required.\n61\t\n62\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n63\t\n64\tEvery edge in the summary diagram has a matching frontmatter `dependencies`\n65\tentry and vice versa (E001→E002, E001→E003, E004→E005, and\n66\tE002/E003/E004/E005/E006→E007). No cycles, no references to nonexistent epics,\n67\tarrows drawn dependency→dependent per the diagram convention. Rule 11 is\n68\tcomplete: the docs-authoring epic E007 is blocked by all five Implementation\n69\tepics. Critical path E001 → E002 → E007 (length 3) fits an M-sized,\n70\tthree-component strategy.\n71\t\n72\tEdge justifications check out against the rules: E001→E002 and E001→E003 are\n73\tRule 3 investigation gates with `gated_by` and `gate_failure_impact` recorded\n74\t(the configuration-matrix portion of E003 genuinely depends on the assessment's\n75\tscale-limit characterization, so the edge is not unjustified serialization).\n76\tE004→E005 is a producer→consumer edge across a team boundary (Rule 12/13).\n77\tThe deliberately absent edges are correctly reasoned: E002↛E004 coupling is\n78\texpressed as an acceptance criterion on E004 rather than an edge, and\n79\tE001↛E004 is justified because HLR-4's scope is stated directly by the\n80\tstrategy. No implementation-type parallelism pairs exist (no konflux-onboarding,\n81\tlicense-validation, or repo-onboarding epics), so Rules 7/8/10 are trivially\n82\tsatisfied. No conditional branch files. Cross-checking epic content against the\n83\tDAG found no uncaptured data/artifact dependencies.\n84\t\n85\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n86\t\n87\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated (Llama Stack/RHOAI GenAI ×4 in\n88\tthree distinct logical changes, TrustyAI/TrustyAI, Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard,\n89\tDocumentation/RHOAI GenAI), and the E004/E005 split correctly applies the\n90\tpassive-component exception (TrustyAI is already GA with no code changes; the\n91\tTrustyAI team validates). The E002 and E001 size overages are kept as single\n92\tlogical changes WITH concrete recorded re-split triggers in the Health Warnings\n93\t(managed trade-offs, within guideline per the rubric).\n94\t\n95\tHowever, there is one major defect with two faces:\n96\t\n97\t- **Work-product type mixing in E004**: E004 bundles code implementation\n98\t  (provider wiring, promotion of the experimental flag) with substantive\n99\t  content authoring — scope item \"Author the integration-side documentation:\n100\t  how to enable and configure TrustyAI guardrails on a GA distribution,\n101\t  supported options, and behavior\" is a new doc topic, not an incidental\n102\t  touch-up falling out of the code change. Code implementation and\n103\t  documentation authoring are different kinds of work with different review\n104\t  criteria and must be separate epics even under one team.\n105\t- **Scope duplication between E004 and E007**: both epics author the TrustyAI\n106\t  guardrails configuration documentation. E004's AC \"Integration documentation\n107\t  exists covering enablement, configuration options, and behavior when the\n108\t  guardrails service is unavailable\" claims the same deliverable as E007's\n109\t  scope bullet \"The TrustyAI guardrails integration as a documented, supported\n110\t  configuration path\" and content outline item 5 (\"Safety guardrails (TrustyAI)\n111\t  configuration\"), published to GA documentation standards under E007's first\n112\t  AC. Two epics producing the same documentation surface is a boundary drawn\n113\t  incorrectly.\n114\t\n115\tCorrection: remove the doc-authoring scope item and its AC from E004 (leave at\n116\tmost an AC that the promoted configuration surface is handed to documentation),\n117\tkeep the guardrails doc topic in E007, and add HLR-4 to E007's HLR Traceability\n118\tso the \"documented integration\" half of HLR-4 remains covered once it leaves\n119\tE004.\n120\t\n121\tA major issue forces this criterion to 0.\n122\t\n123\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n124\t\n125\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the definitive GA scope) rewrites\n126\tthe scope of E002 and E003 — the strategy's own Open Question 1 says the size\n127\tof the stability work is an estimate until it lands, so the answer changes what\n128\tdownstream epics do (Rule 3, not Rule 4). E002 and E003 both set `gated_by:\n129\tRHAISTRAT-9014-E001` with `gate_failure_impact`