{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abd3311ad9eaff32","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014 (Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability)\n11\t\n12\tTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold — the strategy spans three components and\n13\tthree teams (Llama Stack / TrustyAI / Dashboard), six HLRs across P0/P1/P2, and\n14\tcontains a genuine unknown (the GA scope itself is undefined until HLR-1's\n15\tassessment lands). Not docs-only — Llama Stack, Dashboard, and the TrustyAI\n16\tintegration all require code or configuration changes. Full decomposition follows.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment and definitive GA scope | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported Llama Stack distribution integration | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | TrustyAI validation of the guardrails integration for GA distributions | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n29\t\n30\tPriorities: E001 is P0 because it gates all P0 stability work (priority\n31\tinheritance). E002/E003 map directly to P0 HLRs. E007 is a `docs-authoring`\n32\tepic; per the docs-authoring priority exception its priority derives from the\n33\tstrategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0), not from the labels of the\n34\tHLRs it documents (HLR-4 P1, HLR-6 P2), and it does not push priority\n35\tupstream.\n36\t\n37\t## Dependency DAG\n38\t\n39\t```mermaid\n40\tgraph TD\n41\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability & hardening]\n42\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 Support posture]\n43\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 TrustyAI guardrails integration] --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 TrustyAI validation]\n44\t    E002 --> E007[RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 Docs uplift]\n45\t    E003 --> E007\n46\t    E004 --> E007\n47\t    E005 --> E007\n48\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 Dashboard default enablement] --> E007\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tRoots: E001, E004, E006. Critical path: E001 → E002 → E007 (length 3).\n52\t\n53\t## DAG Justification\n54\t\n55\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n56\t|------|------|-----------|\n57\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementations) | The assessment (HLR-1) produces the definitive GA scope; the size and content of the stability/hardening work is explicitly an estimate until it lands (strategy Open Question 1). True gate — `rewrite`: assessment findings change the epic's scope. Unbounded outcome set (open-ended gap inventory), so phased decomposition rather than conditional branches. |\n58\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | The supported configuration matrix in HLR-3 must reflect the scale limits characterized by the assessment; the matrix contents are not knowable before it. True gate — `rewrite` for the configuration-matrix portion. SLO targets and backport policy work could start earlier; the edge is accepted because the epic is one logical change for one team and the assessment is itself P0 and early in the plan. |\n59\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 12/13 (producer → consumer serial; different team boundary, Rule 1) | The TrustyAI team's validation epic consumes the integration built by the Llama Stack side; validation cannot start before the integration exists. Also a team boundary split (RHOAI GenAI vs TrustyAI). |\n60\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Docs describe what was built; always last. |\n61\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n62\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n63\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n64\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n65\t\n66\tDeliberately absent edges:\n67\t\n68\t- **E001 ↛ E004**: the assessment's stated inventory (API stability, upgrade\n69\t  paths, scale limits, error handling) targets the core distribution quality\n70\t  bar, not the TrustyAI guardrails integration, whose GA requirements are\n71\t  stated directly by HLR-4. Serializing E004 on E001 would delay P1 work for\n72\t  no scope benefit.\n73\t- **E002 ↛ E004 and E002 ↛ E006**: different surfaces (guardrails provider\n74\t  wiring; Dashboard feature-flag default). Rule 14/15 — parallel. The coupling\n75\t  that does exist (the integration surface being covered by the stability\n76\t  guarantee) is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E004, not an edge.\n77\t- **E006 has no dependencies**: the Dashboard enablement is a feature-flag\n78\t  default flip on an existing integration surface, independent of the\n79\t  Llama Stack hardening work.\n80\t\n81\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n82\t\n83\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n84\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n85\t| HLR-1 | P0 | GA-readiness assessment inventories gaps (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling), producing definitive GA scope | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Investigation epic; deliverable is the gap inventory and GA scope document. |\n86\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Published API surface carries stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Gated by the assessment (scope-defining). Also absorbs the error-handling/quality-gap closure the assessment inventories — one hardening pass over the same distribution surface; splitting would double-touch the same repos. |\n87\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture: documented SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | One logical change (support posture for one component, one team). |\n88\t| HLR-4 | P1 | TrustyAI guardrails a supported, documented integration for GA distributions | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004, RHAISTRAT-9014-E005, RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | Split by work product and team boundary: RHOAI GenAI builds/promotes the integration in the distribution (E004); the TrustyAI team (passive component — no TrustyAI code changes indicated, TrustyAI is already GA) validates it, which per the passive-component exception generates a separate validation epic owned by that team (E005); the documented-integration half is authored in the docs-authoring epic (E007), which publishes the guardrails configuration topic to GA standards from the configuration surface E004 hands over. |\n89\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters (no TP opt-in gate) | RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Dashboard component, RHOAI Dashboard team. |\n90\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation uplifted to GA standards, including migration notes from TP | RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | `docs-authoring` epic; priority P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical`), exempt from inheritance. |\n91\t\n92\tAll six HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements. E007 maps to HLR-4 (P1)\n93\talongside its primary HLR-6 (P2): the guardrails configuration documentation\n94\tis part of the GA documentation surface this epic authors, so the\n95\tdocumented-integration half of HLR-4 is delivered there rather than in the\n96\tcode epic. This is not a priority-split concern — the docs-authoring epic's\n97\tpriority derives from the strategy frontmatter exception, not from HLR\n98\tlabels, and it does not push priority upstream.\n99\t\n100\t## Health Warnings\n101\t\n102\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n103\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n104\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLR table,\n105\t  Affected Components list, and architecture context. Owning teams were taken\n106\t  from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` (RHOAI GenAI,\n107\t  TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from HLR\n108\t  text rather than strategy ACs.\n109\t- **Documentation epic owner assumed**: the strategy does not name an owner\n110\t  for HLR-6. The epic is assigned to RHOAI GenAI (owner of the component the\n111\t  docs describe) with component `Documentation`; reassign if a dedicated docs\n112\t  team owns this content.\n113\t- **Rule 19 (safety-critical) considered, not applied**: HLR-4 concerns\n114\t  guardrails, but the strategy's core is GA graduation of Llama Stack; the\n115\t  TrustyAI integration promotes an existing integration with an\n116\t  already-GA component rather than designing a new safety mechanism. No\n117\t  fail-mode/security Investigation epics were generated. Flagged so a reviewer\n118\t  can confirm this reading.\n119\t- **Epic size (E002)**: the API stability and GA hardening epic is estimated\n120\t  above the ~2-week guideline — the strategy itself says the size of the\n121\t  stability and hardening work is an estimate until the assessment lands.\n122\t  Kept as one logical change: it is a single hardening pass over one\n123\t  distribution surface (versioning, deprecation policy, upgrade-path testing,\n124\t  assessment-identified gap closure); a split would double-touch the same\n125\t  repos and fragment one mechanical GA-bar pass. **Re-split trigger**: once\n126\t  the GA-readiness assessment lands, re-split E002 if its gap inventory\n127\t  exceeds the stability-mechanics work (versioning/deprecation/upgrade\n128\t  testing) in volume — split the assessment-identified remediation items into\n129\t  separate hardening epics by area at that point.\n130\t- **Epic size (E001)**: the assessment sweeps four inventory areas plus a\n131\t  scattered TP-feedback corpus (support tickets) and may run over a sprint.\n132\t  Kept as one investigation: the deliverable is a single coherent gap\n133\t  inventory and GA scope; splitting would duplicate the evidence gathering.\n134\t  **Re-split trigger**: split the scale-limit characterization into its own\n135\t  investigation if it requires a dedicated perf campaign beyond the\n136\t  assessment's desk-audit scope.\n137\t\n138\t## Tiered Delivery\n139\t\n140\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing exists in this\n141\tstrategy (no upstream PR/RFC, no vendor coordination). The single unknown —\n142\tthe GA scope — is internal and resolved by E001; all downstream work is gated\n143\trather than tiered.\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90be83002d9b1935","agent_id":"a552b5e19b6263c40","session_id":"71dcbc61-9434-456e-a363-a4d82acc6903","uuid":"b58240a5-217c-4826-be79-76bf4eccace5","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:28:21.848Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-abd3311ad9eaff32","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
